# =============================================================================
# cmano-clone monorepo — root .gitattributes
# Unity 6.3 LTS (6000.3.22f1) / ProjectAegis
# =============================================================================
#
# ONE-TIME PER-DEV SETUP (required, or the merge=unityyamlmerge lines below are
# silently inert — git falls back to the default text merge):
#
# STEP 0 — LOCATE UnityYAMLMerge. The path below is a TEMPLATE, NOT A VERIFIED
# FACT. This repo's Unity Hub is not under $HOME (it is installed system-wide,
# probably /opt/unityhub), and Hub's editor-install directory is configurable,
# so the editor root may be ~/Unity/Hub/Editor, /opt/unity/editors,
# /opt/unityhub/editors, or anything set in Hub > Preferences > Installs.
# Find the real one before configuring anything:
#
#   find / -type f -name UnityYAMLMerge 2>/dev/null
#   # or, if you know the editor root:
#   ls -l <editor-root>/6000.3.22f1/Editor/Data/Tools/UnityYAMLMerge
#
#   git config merge.unityyamlmerge.name "Unity SmartMerge"
#   git config merge.unityyamlmerge.driver "<VERIFIED-ABSOLUTE-PATH>/UnityYAMLMerge merge -p --force %O %B %A %A"
#   git config merge.unityyamlmerge.recursive binary
#
#   Template only (adjust the editor root to what `find` actually reported):
#   /home/username01/Unity/Hub/Editor/6000.3.22f1/Editor/Data/Tools/UnityYAMLMerge
#
#   The 6000.5.1f1 install is a different stream and must NOT be used for this
#   driver (its SmartMerge/mergespec can diverge from the pinned editor's
#   serialization).
#
# WHY STEP 0 IS NOT OPTIONAL — a wrong path fails SILENTLY AND DESTRUCTIVELY.
#   `git config` accepts any string; nothing is validated until a merge runs.
#   If the binary is absent, git prints one easily-missed "not found" line, then
#   marks the file conflicted (UU) while leaving the working-tree copy as YOUR
#   side, byte-for-byte, WITH NO CONFLICT MARKERS IN IT. Verified behaviour:
#       $ git merge side
#       .../UnityYAMLMerge: not found
#       CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in f.asset
#       $ git status --porcelain   ->   UU f.asset
#       $ cat f.asset              ->  ours, clean, no <<<<<<< anywhere
#   A dev who sees no markers and runs `git add f.asset` silently discards the
#   entire incoming side of a scene, prefab or ProjectSettings asset. Every
#   .unity/.prefab/.asset merge in this repo is exposed to this.
#
# STEP 0b — SMOKE-TEST the driver before trusting it. In a scratch clone:
#   git checkout -b _drvtest && printf 'a: 1\nb: 2\n' > t.asset
#   git add t.asset && git commit -m t && git checkout -b _drvtest2
#   printf 'a: 9\nb: 2\n' > t.asset && git commit -am t2
#   git checkout _drvtest && printf 'a: 1\nb: 9\n' > t.asset && git commit -am t3
#   git merge _drvtest2
#   PASS = merge succeeds cleanly (a: 9 / b: 9) with no "not found" line.
#   FAIL = any "not found", or a UU file containing no conflict markers.
#   Delete the scratch branches afterwards. Do this once per machine.
#
#   Flags: -p enables per-type fallback tools from
#   .../Editor/Data/Tools/mergespecfile.txt for files SmartMerge cannot parse;
#   --force writes a result with conflict markers instead of aborting the merge.
#   recursive=binary stops the driver from being re-entered during recursive
#   merge-base construction.
#
#   Scope: `git config` (no --global) writes $GIT_DIR/config, which is SHARED by
#   all `git worktree` checkouts under .worktrees/ — configure once in the main
#   clone and every sprint worktree inherits it. Use --global only if you want
#   it across unrelated clones; the editor path is machine-specific either way.
#
#   Prerequisite already satisfied: EditorSettings.asset has
#   m_SerializationMode: 2 (ForceText). SmartMerge cannot operate on binary or
#   mixed serialization — if that setting ever regresses, these rules are dead.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HAZARD 1 — LFS rules are NOT retroactive.
#   Adding filter=lfs below converts files on FUTURE writes only. History is
#   untouched: already-committed PNG/PSD/FBX blobs stay as plain blobs, and
#   every one of them will show as MODIFIED in `git status` (the clean filter
#   emits a pointer that differs from the stored blob) until history is
#   rewritten. Before landing this, inventory what is affected:
#       git ls-files -- '*.png' '*.jpg' '*.tga' '*.psd' '*.fbx' '*.wav' '*.ogg'
#   Converting history is a separate, coordinated exercise (`git lfs migrate
#   import --include=...`), rewrites every SHA, requires a force-push and a
#   re-clone by every dev, and MUST NOT be bundled with this changeset.
#
# HAZARD 2 — landing this rewrites everyone's working tree.
#   The text/eol=lf rules trigger renormalization: the first checkout after this
#   lands can touch thousands of files. Any live Graphite stack rebased across
#   this commit will see EOL-only diffs on every restack, and .worktrees/
#   checkouts renormalize independently. Land it on a quiet trunk, with all
#   stacks either merged or explicitly re-synced (`gt sync` then `gt restack`)
#   immediately after, and announce it. Do not land it mid-sprint.
#
# HAZARD 3 — NEVER run bare `git add --renormalize .` with this file in place.
#   `--renormalize` re-runs CLEAN FILTERS, not just EOL conversion. Because the
#   LFS section below matches *.png/*.psd/*.fbx/*.wav/..., a bare
#   `git add --renormalize .` stages EVERY already-committed media file as an
#   LFS POINTER in one shot — i.e. it performs exactly the mass LFS conversion
#   that HAZARD 1 says must not be bundled with this changeset, and it does so
#   with no prompt and no obvious signal. Verified: a 200 000-byte tracked PNG
#   became a 131-byte pointer blob in the index, with the real object written to
#   .git/lfs/objects, from that single command.
#   If you need to force renormalization, PATHSPEC-LIMIT it to text types, which
#   leaves media blobs untouched (verified — the PNG stayed a 200 000-byte blob):
#       git add --renormalize -- '*.cs' '*.unity' '*.prefab' '*.asset' '*.meta' \
#                                '*.shader' '*.uxml' '*.uss' '*.json' '*.md'
#   Otherwise just let checkout renormalize naturally and do nothing.
#
# PREREQUISITE — git-lfs must be installed and initialised on every machine
#   (`git lfs version`; `git lfs install` once per user) AND the remote must
#   have LFS enabled with quota for the newly-matched media. If git-lfs is
#   absent the filter=lfs attributes are inert no-ops and media commits as
#   plain blobs while everyone believes LFS is on — unrecoverable without a
#   history rewrite. LFS scope here widens from ONE tracked file
#   (aegis_public_corpus.db) to all project media: confirm storage/billing
#   headroom before landing.
# =============================================================================


# --- PRESERVED: existing rules, verbatim -------------------------------------
*.sh text eol=lf
.buildkite/* text eol=lf

# Enterprise public corpus catalog (~65 MB). Do not LFS-track baltic_patrol.db.
assets/data/catalog/aegis_public_corpus.db filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text


# --- Unity YAML assets: SmartMerge -------------------------------------------
# eol=lf is deliberate: SmartMerge is line-oriented, and a CRLF/LF mismatch
# turns any three-way merge into a whole-file conflict. Unity on Linux writes
# LF already; Windows editors tolerate LF working-tree files fine.
*.unity           text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.prefab          text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.asset           text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.mat             text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.anim            text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.controller      text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.overrideController text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.physicMaterial  text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.physicsMaterial2D text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.meta            text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.mask            text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.playable        text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.mixer           text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.terrainlayer    text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.signal          text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.brush           text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.lighting        text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.giparams        text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.spriteatlas     text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.spriteatlasv2   text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.guiskin         text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.flare           text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.fontsettings    text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.prefs           text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.renderTexture   text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.cubemap         text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge
*.shadervariants  text eol=lf merge=unityyamlmerge

# ProjectSettings/*.asset is also Unity YAML and is intentionally covered by
# the *.asset rule above — SmartMerge is the right driver there and this is
# desirable, not accidental. CAVEAT: for ProjectSettings the merge is
# syntactically safe but SEMANTICALLY blind. EditorBuildSettings scene order,
# TagManager layer indices, GraphicsSettings shader-stripping lists and
# InputManager axis order are all position-significant; a clean auto-merge can
# still be wrong. Treat any ProjectSettings/ hunk as requiring human review.
# ProjectVersion.txt is plain text and is deliberately NOT given a merge
# driver — an editor-version bump must always conflict loudly.
#
# PATH ANCHORING: a gitattributes pattern containing a slash is matched relative
# to the directory holding the .gitattributes file. This file lives at the
# MONOREPO ROOT, so a bare `ProjectSettings/ProjectVersion.txt` would only match
# <root>/ProjectSettings/ProjectVersion.txt — which does not exist — and would be
# a dead rule. The real path is under unity/ProjectAegis/. Verified with
# `git check-attr`. Written full-path here; the `**/` form is kept commented for
# any future second Unity project in the monorepo.
unity/ProjectAegis/ProjectSettings/ProjectVersion.txt text eol=lf
# **/ProjectSettings/ProjectVersion.txt text eol=lf

# .meta files: SmartMerge resolves the common case (import-setting drift), but
# a conflict on m_GUID is never mergeable — two assets have claimed one GUID
# and that is a manual, deliberate fix. Never blind-accept a .meta merge.


# --- Addressables ------------------------------------------------------------
# Group/settings assets are YAML and inherit *.asset above. Generated
# catalogs/bins under ServerData|Library are build output, not source.
*.bin  binary
*.hash text eol=lf


# --- Source / config: normalisation + diff drivers ---------------------------
# DECIDE BEFORE LANDING: EditorSettings.asset has m_LineEndingsForNewScripts: 2
# (= Windows/CRLF). The rule below declares eol=lf for the working tree, so every
# script the Editor creates via Create > C# Script is written CRLF and then
# flipped to LF on the next checkout. Git hides this (the clean filter normalises
# CRLF -> LF, so `git status` stays clean), but the two settings disagree and any
# EOL-sensitive tooling or .editorconfig will churn. Either set
# m_LineEndingsForNewScripts to 1 (Unix) in Editor > Preferences, or drop eol=lf
# from *.cs. Not changed here — EditorSettings.asset.NEW in this changeset
# deliberately touches cache-server keys ONLY.
*.cs      text eol=lf diff=csharp
*.rsp     text eol=lf
*.asmdef  text eol=lf
*.asmref  text eol=lf
# diff=csharp is scoped to *.cs on purpose. .asmdef/.asmref are JSON and .uxml
# is XML — applying the C# hunk-header regex to them produces misleading
# @@-context in every diff and review. Not worth the noise.
*.uxml    text eol=lf
# SVG is XML: diffable text. Deliberately NOT LFS.
*.svg             text eol=lf
*.uss     text eol=lf
*.shader  text eol=lf
*.cginc   text eol=lf
*.hlsl    text eol=lf diff=cpp
*.compute text eol=lf
*.shadergraph text eol=lf
*.raytrace text eol=lf

*.csproj      text eol=lf
*.sln         text eol=lf
*.props       text eol=lf
*.targets     text eol=lf
*.json        text eol=lf
*.yml         text eol=lf
*.yaml        text eol=lf
*.md          text eol=lf
*.py          text eol=lf
*.ps1         text eol=lf
*.toml        text eol=lf
*.xml         text eol=lf
*.txt         text eol=lf
*.editorconfig text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
.gitignore     text eol=lf

# packages-lock.json is machine-generated; no union/ours merge is configured
# for it deliberately. A lockfile conflict means two branches moved package
# versions and must be resolved by re-resolving in the editor, not by git.


# --- Git LFS: binary MEDIA only ----------------------------------------------
# Scope is intentionally narrow. NO *.db pattern anywhere — the only
# LFS-tracked database is the explicit aegis_public_corpus.db path above, and
# baltic_patrol.db must stay a normal blob. A blanket *.db would capture it.
# Native binaries (*.dll/*.so/*.a) are also excluded: the ones in this repo are
# gitignored build output, and an LFS pattern would only dirty any that are
# legitimately tracked.

